# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'Each account is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another passage, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it. The study sets Luke 13:6–9 first and the comparison of the children second, and it makes no claim about which was spoken first.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The man and the tree (Luke 13:6). “He spake also this parable; A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard; and he came and sought fruit thereon, and found none.” The sequence has four steps. A certain man has a fig tree. It is planted in his vineyard. He comes and seeks fruit on it. He finds none.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The man’s word to the dresser (13:7). “Then said he unto the dresser of his vineyard, Behold, these three years I come seeking fruit on this fig tree, and find none: cut it down; why cumbereth it the ground?” A second person now appears, “the dresser of his vineyard.” The man gives a count, “these three years.” He says that he comes seeking fruit and finds none. He gives an order, “cut it down,” and a question, “why cumbereth it the ground?”'))
A(('p', '3.3 The dresser’s word to the man (13:8–9). “And he answering said unto him, Lord, let it alone this year also, till I shall dig about it, and dung it: And if it bear fruit, well: and if not, then after that thou shalt cut it down.” The dresser answers the order with a request: let it alone “this year also.” He names what he will do: dig about it and dung it. He then states the outcome two ways. If it bears fruit, “well.” If not, “then after that thou shalt cut it down.”'))
A(('p', '3.4 What follows the story (13:10). Luke does not report the man’s answer, the year, the digging, or the fruit. The next verse reads, “And he was teaching in one of the synagogues on the sabbath.” The story ends on the dresser’s two outcomes and stops there.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 13:6', '“a fig tree planted in his vineyard”; “sought fruit thereon, and found none”', 'A tree in a vineyard; fruit sought; none found.'],
    ['Luke 13:7', '“these three years I come seeking fruit”; “cut it down”', 'A count of years; an order given.'],
    ['Luke 13:8', '“let it alone this year also”; “dig about it, and dung it”', 'A request for one more year, with named care.'],
    ['Luke 13:9', '“if it bear fruit, well”; “if not, then after that thou shalt cut it down”', 'Two outcomes; the text gives neither as having happened.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.5 What the fig tree story contains and leaves unsaid. The text gives a man, a fig tree, a vineyard, a dresser, three years, a seeking, an order, a request for one year, a digging and a dunging, and two possible outcomes. Jesus does not say who the man is. Jesus does not say who the dresser is. Jesus does not say what the fig tree is. Jesus does not say what the vineyard is. Jesus does not say what the fruit is. Jesus does not say what the three years are or what the one more year is. Jesus does not say which outcome came. The story stops with both outcomes still open.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The question of likeness (Matthew 11:16; Luke 7:31). “But whereunto shall I liken this generation?” Luke gives, “And the Lord said, Whereunto then shall I liken the men of this generation? and to what are they like?” In both, Jesus asks the question Himself and goes on to answer it. The one likened is “this generation” in Matthew and “the men of this generation” in Luke.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The children and their words (Matthew 11:16–17; Luke 7:32). “It is like unto children sitting in the markets, and calling unto their fellows, And saying, We have piped unto you, and ye have not danced; we have mourned unto you, and ye have not lamented.” Luke reads, “They are like unto children sitting in the marketplace, and calling one to another, and saying, We have piped unto you, and ye have not danced; we have mourned to you, and ye have not wept.” The picture has children sitting in a public place, one group calling to another. The call has two parts: a piping and no dancing, a mourning and no lamenting (Luke: no weeping).'))
A(('p', '3.8 The sayings about John and the Son of man (Matthew 11:18–19; Luke 7:33–34). “For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, He hath a devil. The Son of man came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners.” Luke has, “For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine; and ye say, He hath a devil. The Son of man is come eating and drinking; and ye say, Behold a gluttonous man, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners!” The text gives two comings and two sayings. John comes neither eating nor drinking, and “He hath a devil” is said. The Son of man comes eating and drinking, and a charge of gluttony and wine-bibbing, and of friendship with publicans and sinners, is said.'))
A(('p', '3.9 The closing saying (Matthew 11:19; Luke 7:35). “But wisdom is justified of her children.” Luke reads, “But wisdom is justified of all her children.” Jesus gives the sentence and goes no further. Matthew then goes to the upbraiding of the cities (11:20), and Luke goes to the Pharisee’s house (7:36).'))
A(('ctbl', 'The two accounts differ in wording at these points.', [
    ['Point', 'Matthew 11', 'Luke 7'],
    ['The one likened', '“this generation”', '“the men of this generation”'],
    ['The place', '“in the markets”', '“in the marketplace”'],
    ['The mourning', '“ye have not lamented”', '“ye have not wept”'],
    ['John', '“For John came neither eating nor drinking”', '“For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine”'],
    ['Who says it', '“they say”', '“ye say”'],
    ['The Son of man', '“came eating and drinking”', '“is come eating and drinking”'],
    ['The closing saying', '“of her children”', '“of all her children”'],
], [0.20, 0.38, 0.42]))
A(('p', '3.10 What the comparison of the children contains and leaves unsaid. The text gives children, a public place, a call with two parts, a reply that does not come, two comings, two sayings and one closing sentence. Jesus does not say who the fellows are that the children call to. Jesus does not say what the piping or the mourning is. Jesus does not say which call belongs to John and which to the Son of man. Jesus does not say what wisdom is. Jesus does not say who the children of wisdom are.'))

