A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify in the comparison of the children. Jesus names the generation, the children it is like, John, and the Son of man. He does not say who the fellows are. He does not say what the piping, the dancing, the mourning or the lamenting is. He does not say which call belongs to which person. He does not say who wisdom is. He does not say who her children are.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Where Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the fig tree is a certain people, that the man or the dresser is a certain person, that the fruit is a certain thing, that the fellows are a certain group, or that wisdom and her children are certain persons is supplying what the Lord did not. Jesus’ words about John and the Son of man are His own statement about that comparison, and they are not carried into the fig tree story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states only the meaning that arises from each comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of either account.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The fig tree story. Luke opens it with “He spake also this parable” (13:6) and does not say that it is a comparison of the kingdom of God. The meaning stated here therefore stays inside the story and its setting. The story shows a search for fruit that finds none, a search that has been repeated for three years, an order that follows, and a request that the tree be left one year more. It ends with two outcomes and does not give either.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the fig tree story communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Fruit is looked for.** The man “came and sought fruit thereon, and found none” (13:6). The story begins with a search and a finding of nothing.',
    '**The search is repeated.** “These three years I come seeking fruit on this fig tree, and find none” (13:7). The finding of none is not a single visit.',
    '**The order is given with a reason.** “Cut it down; why cumbereth it the ground?” (13:7). The reason is stated in the same breath as the order.',
    '**Time is given, and it is limited.** “Let it alone this year also” (13:8) asks for one year and names the care that will be given in it.',
    '**The end is stated in advance.** “If it bear fruit, well: and if not, then after that thou shalt cut it down” (13:9). Both outcomes are said before either happens.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers of Luke 13 had just heard, twice, “except ye repent, ye shall all likewise perish” (13:3, 5). The story set beside those words speaks of fruit sought and not found, of delay given for a time, and of an end that follows if no fruit comes. The text says no more than that about its hearers.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The comparison of the children. Jesus likens the generation He is speaking to, not to a tree, but to children sitting in a public place whose calls bring no answer. The meaning arises from the two sayings that follow. John comes one way and is answered with the charge “He hath a devil.” The Son of man comes the other way and is answered with the charge of a gluttonous man and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. The text shows that whichever way the call came, a charge was found against it.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison of the children communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A call goes out and is not answered.** The children say, “We have piped unto you, and ye have not danced; we have mourned unto you, and ye have not lamented” (Matthew 11:17).',
    '**The generation is not pleased by either coming.** John came “neither eating nor drinking,” and the Son of man came “eating and drinking,” and a charge followed each (11:18–19).',
    '**The charges answer the callers and not the call.** What is said of John and of the Son of man is a charge about the person, as the text gives it.',
    '**Jesus closes with a saying and does not explain it.** “But wisdom is justified of her children” (11:19) is the sentence on which the comparison ends.',
]))
A(('p', '5.6 What the two accounts support, in short statements. Fruit is looked for, and its absence is stated. Time is given and it has an end. A refusal can find a charge against any messenger. These statements arise from the accounts and do not say that the tree, the vineyard, the children or wisdom represents anything.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say who the man or the dresser is, or what the fig tree, the vineyard or the fruit is. It does not say who the fellows are or who wisdom’s children are. It does not say which outcome of the fig tree story came. It does not turn fruit-bearing, repenting, piping or dancing into steps by which a person is saved. It does not draw a meaning from another parable into either account.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The fig tree of Luke 13 and the children of Matthew 11 and Luke 7 stand beside several other passages that use a fig tree, a generation, a refusal of John or a question about eating and drinking. Each is kept apart here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 24:32–33; Mark 13:28–29; Luke 21:29–31', '“Now learn a parable of the fig tree”', 'The Fig Tree Lesson, spoken about the leaves of a fig tree and the nearness of summer. No fruit sought, no owner, no dresser, no order to cut down.'],
    ['Luke 3:9; Matt 3:10', '“every tree therefore which bringeth not forth good fruit is hewn down, and cast into the fire”', 'A saying of John, spoken to those who came to his baptism. It is not an explanation of Luke 13:7–9.'],
    ['Matt 21:28–32', '“John came unto you in the way of righteousness, and ye believed him not”', 'The Two Sons. A vineyard and a father, a question put to the chief priests and elders, and Jesus’ own application of John’s coming. A different parable and a different setting.'],
    ['Matt 9:14–17; Luke 5:33–39', '“Why do the disciples of John fast often”', 'The New Cloth and New Wineskins. A question about fasting and an answer from Jesus. The eating and drinking of Luke 7:34 is a charge made against the Son of man, not an answer to that question.'],
    ['Luke 11:29–32; Matt 12:38–45', '“This is an evil generation”', 'Other sayings about this generation, each given where a sign was asked. They are not an explanation of the children in the market.'],
], [0.22, 0.30, 0.48]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The fig tree is told in Luke 13 after a report about the Galilæans and before a sabbath in a synagogue. The comparison of the children is told after John’s question, in Matthew 11 before the upbraiding of the cities and in Luke 7 before a Pharisee’s house. A shared word such as “fruit,” “generation” or “John” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The fig tree story has a man, a dresser and a tree. The comparison of the children has children and their fellows, with John and the Son of man named in Jesus’ sayings. The Fig Tree Lesson has a fig tree and those who watch it. The Two Sons has a father and two sons. No character is carried from one account into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The fig tree story moves from a search that fails, through an order and a plea, to two open outcomes. The comparison of the children moves from a picture, through two sayings, to a closing sentence. The Fig Tree Lesson moves from leaves to the nearness of summer. None of those movements is read into another.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the tree of Luke 13:6 is a fig tree planted in a vineyard and sought for fruit, and it is not the fig tree whose leaves are learned from in Matthew 24:32. Second, the words said of John in Matthew 11:18 are not the words Jesus says of John in Matthew 21:32, which stand in the Two Sons. Third, the “eating and drinking” of the Son of man in Luke 7:34 is not the question about eating and drinking in Luke 5:33.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17) and sent the Twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (10:5–7). Luke records that He preached the kingdom of God and sent the Twelve to do the same (4:43; 9:2). The fig tree story and the comparison of the children belong to that ministry. The hearers are the people of those scenes: those who told Jesus of the Galilæans, the multitudes and the people who heard Him speak of John.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. These accounts were spoken before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in the fig tree story or in the comparison of the children speaks of it. The tree, the vineyard, the generation, the children and wisdom’s children are not the Body of Christ.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The fig tree, the fruit, the cutting down, the piping, the mourning and the charges are parts of a story and a comparison. They are not the identity of the Body and they are not its salvation. Repentance, as Jesus calls for it in Luke 13:3 and 13:5, belongs to the message of the kingdom spoken to the hearers of that day, and it is not laid on the Body as a condition of salvation. The accounts are not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in these passages: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what these accounts show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson about time.** The man gives a year more, and the dresser states the end in advance. A reader learns that time given is not time without limit.',
    '**A lesson about fruit.** The man seeks fruit and says plainly that he finds none. A reader learns to look for what is actually there and to say so.',
    '**A lesson about care.** The dresser names what he will do in the year asked for. A reader learns that a request for more time is joined to work to be done in it.',
    '**A lesson about fault-finding.** A charge was found against John and a charge against the Son of man. A reader learns to ask whether a complaint is a reason or a way of refusing a call.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The fig tree’s outcomes are not turned into the Body’s hope or warning, and the generation’s refusal is not turned into a description of the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in these passages.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells of a man who comes three years to a fig tree in his vineyard and finds no fruit. The man orders it cut down, and the dresser asks for one year more, with digging and dung, and names both outcomes: fruit, and well; no fruit, and then the tree is cut down. The story stops there. Jesus also likens this generation to children in the market who call and are not answered, and says that John came neither eating nor drinking and was called possessed, and that the Son of man came eating and drinking and was called a glutton and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. He closes with the sentence, “But wisdom is justified of her children.” Jesus gives no explanation of the fig tree and names none of its parts. He names the generation, the children, John and the Son of man in the comparison, and no one else. The fig tree says that fruit is looked for, that time is given, and that the end is stated. The comparison says that a charge was found against each coming. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the tree, the man, the dresser, the fellows, wisdom or her children are, or that makes fruit-bearing or repenting a way the Body is saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** fruit sought and not found for three years; an order to cut down; a plea for one year more with digging and dung; two outcomes stated and neither given. Children in the market whose calls get no answer; John and the Son of man, each met with a charge; a closing sentence about wisdom.',
    '**The meaning:** fruit is looked for and its absence is said plainly; time is given and it has an end; a refusal can find a charge against any messenger.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus for the fig tree; no identity for the man, the dresser, the tree, the vineyard or the fruit; no identity for the fellows, wisdom or her children; the Fig Tree Lesson, the Two Sons and the New Cloth and New Wineskins are not carried in.',
    '**The present lesson:** time, fruit, care and fault-finding, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between characters.** The man and the dresser are two persons in the fig tree story, and Jesus names neither. The children and their fellows in the market are not the tree’s people. John and the Son of man are named by Jesus in His own sayings and not in the picture.',
    '**Between actions.** The man seeks fruit and orders the tree cut down; the dresser asks for a year and names his care. The children pipe and mourn; the generation does not dance or lament; a charge is made against each coming.',
    '**Between settings.** Luke 13:6 follows the report of the Galilæans and two calls to repent. Matthew 11:16 and Luke 7:31 follow John’s question and what Jesus says of John.',
    '**Between these accounts and their neighbors.** The leaves of the Fig Tree Lesson are not the unfruitful tree. The Two Sons’ application of John is not the charge “He hath a devil.” The question about fasting is not the charge of eating and drinking.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the man, the dresser, the tree, the vineyard, the fruit, the fellows, wisdom or her children. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the Fig Tree Lesson, the saying of John in Luke 3:9, the Two Sons, the New Cloth and New Wineskins, or the other sayings about this generation.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Seeking fruit, bearing fruit, repenting, piping and dancing are parts of a story and a comparison. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

